"Internal Error ()\nciteturn703view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ams.org/notices/201510/rnoti-p1214.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.ams.org/notices/201510/rnoti-p1214.pdf: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/documents/publications/ILsp15_0.pdf)\nciteturn703view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/documents/publications/ILsp15_0.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2259\nL0@P1: Many natural and social phenomena may be\nL1@P1: viewed as inherently computational; they evolve\nL2@P1: patterns of information that can be described algorith\u0002mically and studied through computational models\nL3@P1: and techniques. A workshop on the computational\nL4@P1: lens, organized by Avi Wigderson, Herbert H. Maass\nL5@P1: Professor in the School of Mathematics, highlighted\nL6@P1: the state-of-art and future challenges of this interac\u0002tion of computational theory with physics, social\nL7@P1: sciences, economics, and biology.\nL8@P1: Leslie Valiant, T. Jefferson Coolidge Professor of\nL9@P1: Computer Science and Applied Mathematics in the\nL10@P1: School of Engineering and Applied Sciences at\nL11@P1:  Harvard University, spoke on the scientific question\nL12@P1: of how to determine the molecular mechanism of\nL13@P1: biological evolution, to a level of specificity that it\nL14@P1: can be simulated by a computer, and to understand\nL15@P1: why this mechanism can do the remarkable things\nL16@P1: that it has done within the time that has been avail\u0002able. Valiant’s talk addressed how the tools needed\nL17@P1: to approach this come from machine learning, the\nL18@P1: field that studies how mechanisms that achieve com\u0002plex functionality can arise by a process of adaptation\nL19@P1: rather than design.\nL20@P1: Tim Roughgarden, Associate Professor of Computer\nL21@P1: Science and (by courtesy) Management Science and\nL22@P1: Engineering at Stanford University, discussed a num\u0002ber of tools that theoretical computer science offers to\nL23@P1: reason about economic problems in novel ways. For\nL24@P1: example, complexity theory sheds new light on the\nL25@P1: “bounded rationality” of decision-makers; approxima\u0002tion guarantees, originally developed to analyze\nL26@P1: heuristics, can be usefully applied to Nash equilibria;\nL27@P1: and computationally efficient algorithms are an essen\u0002tial ingredient to modern, large-scale auction designs.\nL28@P1: Jon Kleinberg, Tisch University Professor in the\nL29@P1: Departments of Computer Science and Information\nL30@P1: Science at Cornell University, spoke about computa\u0002tional phenomena in social interaction. With an\nL31@P1: increasing amount of social interaction taking place\nL32@P1: in the digital domain, and often in public online set\u0002tings, we are accumulating enormous amounts of data\nL33@P1: about phenomena that were once essentially invisible\nL34@P1: to us: the collective behavior and social interactions\nL35@P1: of hundreds of millions of people, recorded at\nL36@P1: unprecedented levels of scale and resolution. Klein\u0002berg reviewed how modeling and analyzing these phe\u0002nomena computationally offers new insights into the\nL37@P1: design of online applications, as well as new perspec\u0002tives on fundamental questions in the social sciences.\nL38@P1: Scott Aaronson, Associate Professor of Electrical\nL39@P1: Engineering and Computer Science at the Massachu\u0002setts Institute of Technology, discussed the quest to\nL40@P1: understand the limits of efficient computation in the\nL41@P1: physical universe, and how that quest has been giving\nL42@P1: us new insights into physics over the last two decades.\nL43@P1: He explored the following questions: Can scalable\nL44@P1: quantum computers be built? Can they teach us any\u0002thing new about physics? Is there some new physical\nL45@P1: principle that explains why they cannot be built?\nL46@P1: What would quantum computers be good for? Can\nL47@P1: quantum computing help us resolve which interpreta\u0002tion of quantum mechanics is the right one? Which\nL48@P1: systems in nature can be universal computers, and\nL49@P1: which cannot? Aaronson also described a striking\nL50@P1: recent application of computational complexity theory\nL51@P1: to the black hole information loss problem. ■\nL52@P1: When Professor Sabine Schmidtke\nL53@P1: and Hassan Ansari, an Iranian\nL54@P1: national, met more than a decade ago\nL55@P1: in Tehran, Ansari was a student of\nL56@P1: the traditional religious system in\nL57@P1: Qum and Tehran (the “Hawza”).\nL58@P1: Ansari had read Schmidtke’s doctoral\nL59@P1: thesis The Theology of al-‘Allāma al\u0002H. illī (d. 726/1325), which was trans\u0002lated into Persian and published in\nL60@P1: Iran in 1999. Schmidtke’s scholarship\nL61@P1: changed Ansari’s approach to Islamic\nL62@P1: sources and was one of the reasons why he became interested in historical studies on\nL63@P1: Islamic theology. “The historical approach is not only useful, it is necessary,” says\nL64@P1: Ansari. “I talk now as a Muslim scholar. We need to have this kind of historical studies\nL65@P1: to change our approach to our own intellectual and legal tradition and its holy texts.”\nL66@P1: What makes Ansari a particularly exceptional scholar is his combination of\nL67@P1: Western and traditionalist Islamic training. In the “Hawza” in Qum and Tehran, he\nL68@P1: Lens of Computation on the Sciences\nL69@P1: What do quantum interference, flocking of birds, Facebook communities, and stock prices have in common?\nL70@P1: Sights from a Field Trip in the Milky Way:\nL71@P1: From Paleoclimatology to Dark Matter\nL72@P1: How might climate be influenced by cosmic rays?\nL73@P1: Institute for Advanced Study\nL74@P1: (Continued on page 14) (Continued on page 5)\nL75@P1: Spring 2015\nL76@P1: Einstein Drive, Princeton, New Jersey 08540 www.ias.edu\nL77@P1: BY NIR SHAVIV\nL78@P1: I\nL79@P1: n 1913, Victor Hess measured the\nL80@P1: background level of atmospheric\nL81@P1: ionization while ascending with a\nL82@P1: balloon. By doing so, he discovered\nL83@P1: that Earth is continuously bathed\nL84@P1: in ionizing radiation. These cosmic\nL85@P1: rays primarily consist of protons\nL86@P1: and heavier nuclei with energies\nL87@P1: between their rest mass and a trillion\nL88@P1: times larger. In 1934, Walter Baade\nL89@P1: and Fritz Zwicky suggested that cos\u0002mic rays originate from supernovae,\nL90@P1: the explosive death of massive stars.\nL91@P1: However, only in 2013 was it directly proved, using gamma-ray observations with\nL92@P1: the Fermi satellite, that cosmic rays are indeed accelerated by supernova remnants.\nL93@P1: Thus, the amount of ionization in the lower atmosphere is almost entirely governed\nL94@P1: The Necessity of a Historical\nL95@P1: Approach to Islamic Theology\nL96@P1: Tracing modern Islamic thought to the Middle Ages\nL97@P1: Our galactic journey imprinted in the climate—when\nL98@P1: Earth’s temperature (red dots warm, blue dots cold)\nL99@P1: is plotted as a function of time (vertical axis) and as a\nL100@P1: function of time folded over a 32- million-year period\nL101@P1: (horizontal axis), the 32-million-year oscillation of the\nL102@P1: solar system relative to the galactic plane is evident.\nL103@P1: DAN KING\nL104@P1: IASThe InstituteLetter\nL105@P1: Hassan Ansari (right) with Sabine Schmidtke\nL106@P1: NIR SHAVIV\nL107@P1: Recommended Viewing: Conference talks\nL108@P1: may be viewed at https://video.ias.edu/\nL109@P1-2: computationconference/2014/1122.ANGELOS CHANIOTIS, Professor in the School\nL110@P2: of Historical Studies, has received an Anne -\nL111@P2: liese Maier Research Award from the Alexander von\nL112@P2: Humboldt Foundation. Additionally, Chaniotis has\nL113@P2: been appointed to the Board of Directors of the\nL114@P2: Alexander S. Onassis Public Benefit Foundation.\nL115@P2: q\nL116@P2: Brill Academic Publishers has published Manufactur\u0002ing a Past for the Present: Forgery and Authenticity in\nL117@P2: Medievalist Texts and Objects in Nine teenth- Century Europe\nL118@P2: (2014). Edited by PATRICK J. GEARY, Professor in the\nL119@P2: School of Historical Studies, János M. Bak, and Gábor\nL120@P2: Klaniczay, this series of essays explores the volatile\nL121@P2: notion of authenticity in nineteenth-century Europe.\nL122@P2: q\nL123@P2: J\nL124@P2: ONATHAN ISRAEL, Andrew W. Mellon Professor\nL125@P2: in the School of Historical Studies, has received a\nL126@P2: 2015 PROSE award for Revolutionary Ideas: An Intellec\u0002tual History of the French Revolution from The Rights of\nL127@P2: Man to Robespierre (Princeton University Press, 2014).\nL128@P2: q\nL129@P2: Oxford University Press has published Towards\nL130@P2: a Better Global Economy: Policy Implications for\nL131@P2:  Citizens Worldwide in the 21st Century (2014) by\nL132@P2: DANI RODRIK, Albert O. Hirschman Professor in\nL133@P2: the School of Social Science, and six additional\nL134@P2: authors. The book examines the factors that are most\nL135@P2: likely to facilitate the process of beneficial economic\nL136@P2: growth in low-, middle-, and high-income countries.\nL137@P2: q\nL138@P2: SABINE SCHMIDTKE, Professor in the School of\nL139@P2: Historical Studies, is the founding editor-in-chief\nL140@P2: of a new peer-reviewed journal, Intellectual History of\nL141@P2: the Islamicate World (Brill), that bridges the disciplines\nL142@P2: of Islamic, Jewish, and Eastern Christian Studies.\nL143@P2: q\nL144@P2: Berghahn Books has published Clausewitz in His\nL145@P2: Time: Essays in the Cultural and Intellectual History\nL146@P2: of Thinking about War (2014) by PETER PARET, Pro\u0002fessor Emeritus in the School of Historical Studies.\nL147@P2: The work follows the career of Prussian general and\nL148@P2: military theorist Carl von Clausewitz (1780–1831)\nL149@P2: and explores the late-eighteenth- century thought\nL150@P2: that helped shape his analytic method.\nL151@P2: q\nL152@P2: MARTIN L. LEIBOWITZ, Vice Chairman of the\nL153@P2: Institute’s Board, was awarded the 2014 IAQF/\nL154@P2: SunGard Financial Engineer of the Year Award in\nL155@P2: recognition of his contributions to quantitative finance.\nL156@P2: q\nL157@P2: DAVID M. RUBENSTEIN, Institute Trustee, was\nL158@P2: awarded the Museum of American Finance’s 2015\nL159@P2: Whitehead Award for Distinguished Public Service and\nL160@P2: Financial Leadership. Rubenstein also received the\nL161@P2: Susan G. Komen Breast Cancer Foundation’s Lifetime\nL162@P2: Achieve ment Award in recognition of his support of\nL163@P2: research and global and community initiatives.\nL164@P2: q\nL165@P2: CHARLES SIMONYI, Chairman of the Institute’s\nL166@P2: Board, has been named a Stanford University\nL167@P2: Engineering Hero for creating the Bravo WYSIWYG\nL168@P2: text editor in 1974, when he was at Xerox PARC, and\nL169@P2: for his role as chief architect of Micro soft Word,\nL170@P2: Excel, and other widely used programs.\nL171@P2: q\nL172@P2: MANDUHAI BUYANDELGER, Member in the\nL173@P2: School of Social Science, has received the 2014\nL174@P2: Francis L.K. Hsu Book Prize from the Society for East\nL175@P2: Asian Anthropology for Tragic Spirits: Shamanism,\nL176@P2: Memory, and Gender in Contemporary Mongolia\nL177@P2: (Chicago University Press, 2013).\nL178@P2: q\nL179@P2: RUSH HOLT, Director’s Visitor, has been named\nL180@P2: Chief Executive Officer of the American Asso\u0002ciation for the Advancement of Science.\nL181@P2: q\nL182@P2: The 2015 Dannie Heineman Prize in Astrophysics\nL183@P2: was awarded to DAVID SPERGEL, Visitor in\nL184@P2: the School of Natural Sciences, and MARC\nL185@P2: KAMIONKOWSKI, former Member (1991–95) in\nL186@P2: School of Mathematics, for their investigation of the\nL187@P2: flunctuations of the cosmic microwave background—\nL188@P2: work done while Kamionkowski was a Member. Also,\nL189@P2: Kamionkowski was named a 2014 Simons Investigator.\nL190@P2: q\nL191@P2: RASHID SUNYAEV, Visiting Professor in the\nL192@P2: School of Natural Sciences, has received the\nL193@P2: Eddington Medal from the Royal Astronomical Soci\u0002ety for predicting the kinetic Sunyaev-Zeldovich\nL194@P2: effect, which is used to measure the velocity of galaxy\nL195@P2: clusters relative to the cosmic background radiation.\nL196@P2: Sunyaev was also named the first recipient of the\nL197@P2:  Zeldovich Gold Medal from the Russian Academy of\nL198@P2: Sciences.\nL199@P2: q\nL200@P2: The New Horizons in Physics Prize was awarded to\nL201@P2: HORACIO CASINI and MARINA HUERTA,\nL202@P2: former Visitors (2014) in the School of Natural\nL203@P2: Sciences, for fundamental ideals about entropy in\nL204@P2: quantum field theory and quantum gravity, and\nL205@P2: PHILIP SCHUSTER and NATALIA TORO, former\nL206@P2: Members (2011) in the School, for their work on pio\u0002neering the simplified models framework for new\nL207@P2: physics searches at the Large Hadron Collider.\nL208@P2: q\nL209@P2: STANLEY DESER, former Member (1953–55,\nL210@P2: 1993–94) in the School of Natural Sciences, was\nL211@P2: awarded the Einstein Society’s 2015 Einstein Medal\nL212@P2: for his contributions to general relativity, in particu\u0002lar the development of ADM formalism.\nL213@P2: MAYKE BRECHTJE DE JONG, former Member\nL214@P2: (2012) in the School of Historical Studies, has\nL215@P2: received a Research Award from the Alexander von\nL216@P2: Humboldt Foundation to complete the book she\nL217@P2: began at IAS on the interconnectedness of public\nL218@P2: and religious domains in late antiquity and the early\nL219@P2:  Middle Ages.\nL220@P2: q\nL221@P2: SUBHASH KHOT, former Member (2003–04)\nL222@P2: in the School of Mathematics, has received\nL223@P2: the 2014 Rolf Nevanlinna Prize from the International\nL224@P2: Mathematical Union for outstanding contributions in\nL225@P2: mathematical aspects of information sciences.\nL226@P2: q\nL227@P2: JEFFREY LAGARIAS, former Visitor (2000, 2001)\nL228@P2: in the School of Mathematics, received the AMS\nL229@P2: 2015 Levi L. Conant Prize jointly with Chuanming\nL230@P2: Zong for their article “Mysteries in Packing Regular\nL231@P2: Tetrahedra” (Notices of the AMS, December 2012).\nL232@P2: q\nL233@P2: ROBERT K. LAZARSFELD, former Member (1981–\nL234@P2: 82) in the School of Mathematics, was awarded\nL235@P2: the AMS 2015 Leroy P. Steele Prize for Mathematical\nL236@P2: Exposition for his work Positivity in Algebraic Geometry\nL237@P2: I and II (Springer, 2004).\nL238@P2: q\nL239@P2: J\nL240@P2: OEL LEBOWITZ, former Member in the Schools\nL241@P2: of Mathematics (2001–02, 2006, 2013–14) and\nL242@P2: Natural Sciences (1980–81), has received the 2014\nL243@P2: Grande Médaille from the Académie des Sciences of\nL244@P2: the Institut de France, their highest honor.\nL245@P2: q\nL246@P2: BRICE MÉNARD, former Member (2003–06) in\nL247@P2: the School of Natural Sciences, has been\nL248@P2: awarded the 2014 David and Lucile Packard Founda\u0002tion Fellowship for Science and Engineering, which\nL249@P2: provides flexible funding to a group of scientists and\nL250@P2: engineers who have demonstrated vision for the\nL251@P2: future of their fields and for the betterment of society.\nL252@P2: q\nL253@P2: VANESSA OGLE, former Member (2013–14) in\nL254@P2: the School of Social Science, has received the\nL255@P2: Social Science History Association’s President’s\nL256@P2: Book Award for her first book, Contesting Time: The\nL257@P2: Global Struggle for Uniformity and Its Unintended\nL258@P2:  Consequences, 1870s–1950s, forthcoming in fall 2015\nL259@P2: from Harvard University Press.\nL260@P2: q\nL261@P2: HEE OH, former Visitor (2006) and Member\nL262@P2: (2002–03) in the School of Mathematics,\nL263@P2: received the AMS 2015 Ruth Lyttle Satter Prize for\nL264@P2: her fundamental contributions to the dynamics on\nL265@P2: homogeneous spaces, discrete subgroups of Lie\nL266@P2: groups, and applications to number theory.\nL267@P2: q\nL268@P2: SAUL PERLMUTTER, former Member (2011) in\nL269@P2: the School of Natural Sciences, received the\nL270@P2: Breakthrough Prize in Fundamental Physics, which\nL271@P2: recognizes major insights into the deepest questions\nL272@P2: of the universe. JOHN TONRY, former Member\nL273@P2: (1980–82) in the School, is a member of the High-Z\nL274@P2: Supernovae Search Team that also won the Break\u0002through Prize in Fundamental Physics.\nL275@P2: q\nL276@P2: NIKHIL SRIVASTAVA, former Visitor (2012) and\nL277@P2: Member (2010–11) in the School of Mathematics,\nL278@P2: won the Society for Industrial and Applied Mathe\u0002matics’2014 George Pólya Prize for solving a fifty-year\u0002old problem, the Kadison-Singer conjecture.\nL279@P2:  Co n t e n t s\nL280@P2: Questions and comments regarding the Institute Letter should be\nL281@P2: directed to Kelly Devine Thomas, Senior Publications Officer, via\nL282@P2: email at kdthomas@ias.edu or by telephone at (609) 734-8091.\nL283@P2: Issues of the Institute Letter and other Institute publications are\nL284@P2: available online at www.ias.edu/about/publications/.\nL285@P2: Articles from the Institute Letter are available online at\nL286@P2: www.ias.edu/about/publications/ias-letter/articles/.\nL287@P2: To receive monthly updates on Institute events, videos,\nL288@P2: and other news by email, subscribe to IAS eNews at\nL289@P2: www.ias.edu/news/enews-subscription/.\nL290@P2: News of the Institute Community\nL291@P2: 2\nL292@P2: 2 News of the Institute Community\nL293@P2: 3 Recent Gifts in Support of Memberships and Campus\nL294@P2: Programs at IAS\nL295@P2:  Curiosities: Light’s Revelations and Mysteries\nL296@P2: 4 How Do You Say “Bastard” in Medieval Latin?\nL297@P2: 5 The IAS Questionnaire: David Spergel\nL298@P2: 6 Islamic Law and Private International Law: The Case\nL299@P2: of International Child Abduction\nL300@P2: 7 Renegade Dreams\nL301@P2: 8 Birth of a Theorem\nL302@P2: 10 Music Theory’s Monstrous Chord\nL303@P2: 11 Panofsky’s Suppressed Treatise on Michelangelo\nL304@P2: 12 Memories of the Institute for Advanced Study\nL305@P2: 15 Save the Date: America’s First National Math Festival\nL306@P2:  New Leadership Appointments\nL307@P2-3:  Murph Goldberger (1922–2014)BY ROBBERT DIJKGRAAF\nL308@P3: Light is the great unifier. John Wheeler, the beloved Princeton physicist, used to\nL309@P3: draw the universe as a big capital U with a little eye on one leg, signifying that\nL310@P3: we, human beings, are the eyes of the universe looking back at itself. The universe\nL311@P3: after many, many billions of years formed human life on planet Earth, and we use\nL312@P3: light to observe and understand the universe.\nL313@P3: The growing understanding of the nature of light through the centuries is the\nL314@P3: perfect metaphor for science: it is an eye-opener. Almost 350 years ago, Isaac New\u0002ton, as a young man, put a prism in a beam of light and unraveled its various colors.\nL315@P3: This was the beginning of a long story. Around the year 1800, the astronomer\nL316@P3: William Herschel was the first to measure the tempera\u0002ture of light. He made the startling discovery that the\nL317@P3: rainbow does not stop at red, but actually continues,\nL318@P3: invisibly, as infrared light, which we cannot see but can\nL319@P3: feel as a sensation of warmth.\nL320@P3: What is light? Physics has a simple answer: an elec\u0002tromagnetic wave. Exactly 150 years ago, the Scottish\nL321@P3: physicist James Clerk Maxwell discovered the laws that\nL322@P3: describe these waves. I have a T-shirt with these equa\u0002tions and the text “And then there was light.” If only\nL323@P3: Maxwell had patented his equations! It would be\nL324@P3: enough to finance all research in the world.\nL325@P3: Maxwell’s theory is the first and finest example of\nL326@P3: unification: electricity and magnetism bound together\nL327@P3: in a single formula. There is a good reason his portrait\nL328@P3: hangs in Einstein’s study, together with Michael Fara\u0002day’s. Suddenly, light was connected to lightning, batter\u0002ies, and magnets. Now we can add to this radio,\nL329@P3: television, wireless internet, microwaves, X-rays, and the\nL330@P3: complete high-tech industry.\nL331@P3: We have learned that the rainbow continues on both ends, in theory even infi\u0002nitely far. On one end, electromagnetic radiation extends to the very large, from\nL332@P3: microwaves to the radio waves that curl around the Earth. On the other end, we\nL333@P3: find the ultraviolet that birds can see, the X-rays that penetrate our bodies, and the\nL334@P3: gamma rays that can spontaneously produce particles.\nL335@P3: Light is the connector between all the physical elements in our universe, and it\nL336@P3: explains the most basic structures in life. Grass is green because of the way light is\nL337@P3: absorbed by the chlorophyll molecules in its leaves. Even space and time itself are\nL338@P3: illuminated by light. One hundred years ago, in November 1915, Einstein discov\u0002ered his theory of general relativity. He realized that if you put anything—literally\nL339@P3: anything—in space, space and time will curve, and that gravity manifests itself\nL340@P3: through these curvatures. The famous 1919 experiment, which proved his theory\nL341@P3: and made him famous, used a solar eclipse to measure the warping of starlight\nL342@P3: through the sun’s gravitational field.\nL343@P3: With modern telescopes, we now see this effect in full glory in the sky. We\nL344@P3: observe how galaxies act as cosmic lenses and distort the images of stars and other\nL345@P3: galaxies in the most bizarre ways. But the most dramatic way in which the universe\nL346@P3: shapes light is through its expansion. Astronomers have discerned this phenome\u0002non by noticing that the colors of distant stars are shifted toward the red. Objects\nL347@P3: that are moving have different colors—this is known as the Doppler effect—and\nL348@P3: some of these galaxies move away from us so fast that they are in the deep infrared.\nL349@P3: So the shape of space and time literally colors our universe.\nL350@P3: This coloring is most spectacular in the case of the universe’s first light. This\nL351@P3: light was emitted roughly 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when matter was no\nL352@P3: longer closely tied together and light could escape. It\nL353@P3: has been traveling through the universe ever since and\nL354@P3: can now be detected as microwave radiation. That sig\u0002nal was first detected fifty years ago—the third anniver\u0002sary that makes 2015 the International Year of Light.\nL355@P3: Satellites now produce detailed maps of this primordial\nL356@P3: light, beautiful pointillist paintings cast by the universe.\nL357@P3: In fact, light allows us to connect to the very begin\u0002ning of the universe. By carefully looking at the uni\u0002verse’s first light, we hope to detect the primordial\nL358@P3: gravitational waves or ripples out of which the universe\nL359@P3: began. A cosmological theory called inflation tells us\nL360@P3: that our universe started as a miniature speck of space\nL361@P3: that expanded to gigantic dimensions. Inflation theory\nL362@P3: is being tested right now by trying to find these swirls or\nL363@P3: fluctuations in the universe’s pointillist painting. Last\nL364@P3: year, a team of researchers announced that they had\nL365@P3: detected evidence of these quantum fluctuations.\nL366@P3: While these apparent signals turned out to be galaxy\nL367@P3: dust, researchers keep looking for these signatures.\nL368@P3: These days, Wheeler’s image is more appropriate than ever. By observing light\nL369@P3: in all its manifestations we can make contact with worlds that are at the outer edge\nL370@P3: and the very beginning of our cosmos. For the first time in the history of science,\nL371@P3: we look through our biggest telescopes or biggest microscopes, peering to the end\nL372@P3: of the universe or the most inner parts of subnuclear particles, and essentially\nL373@P3: search for the same thing: What is the universe made of?\nL374@P3: There are many beautiful ways that architects, designers, and artists shape light.\nL375@P3: But the universe might be the greatest artist, and nature is at least as imaginative\nL376@P3: as we are. ■\nL377@P3: This article was drawn from a keynote speech that Robbert Dijkgraaf, Institute Director\nL378@P3: and Leon Levy Professor, delivered at the International Year of Light 2015 kick-off event\nL379@P3: in Amsterdam: www.ias.edu/ias-letter/2015/video/dijkgraaf-light. For more information\nL380@P3: about the International Year of Light, a global U.N. initiative, visit www.light2015.org.\nL381@P3: 3\nL382@P3: Recent Gifts in Support of Memberships and Campus Programs at IAS\nL383@P3: ERIC AND WENDY SCHMIDT GIVE $5 MILLION\nL384@P3: Eric Schmidt, Executive Chairman of Google Inc.\nL385@P3: and Trustee of the Institute for Advanced Study,\nL386@P3: and Wendy Schmidt, President of the Schmidt Fam\u0002ily Foundation, have donated $5 million to support\nL387@P3: four Members, known as Schmidt Fellows, across the\nL388@P3: Schools of Natural Sciences and Mathematics and\nL389@P3: to support campus programs and promotion. The $5\nL390@P3: million gift from the Schmidts is an important con\u0002tribution to the Campaign for the Institute, which\nL391@P3: ends on June 30, 2015, and helps to complete the\nL392@P3: $100 million campaign challenge established by the\nL393@P3: Simons Foundation and the Charles and Lisa\nL394@P3: Simonyi Fund for Arts and Sciences.\nL395@P3: The Schmidts’ gift will support the world’s top\nL396@P3: early-stage talent in mathematics and the natural\nL397@P3: sciences with the time, space, and conducive envi\u0002ronment to push the boundaries of human under\u0002standing. It will also be used to promote the Institute’s\nL398@P3: work through the convening of campus programs\nL399@P3: designed to draw key audiences, including academics,\nL400@P3: philanthropists, thought leaders, and media outlets.\nL401@P3: “Not only does the Institute bring together aca\u0002demics of the highest level, but it is a symbol for the\nL402@P3: importance of academic freedom and basic research,”\nL403@P3: said Robbert Dijkgraaf, Director and Leon Levy\nL404@P3:  Professor. “This generous gift from Wendy and Eric\nL405@P3: allows us the freedom and independence to draw\nL406@P3: exciting young talent and cultivate programs on\nL407@P3: campus for existing and new audiences.” ■\nL408@P3: INFOSYS GIVES $2 MILLION\nL409@P3: I\nL410@P3: n October, Infosys and the Institute announced a\nL411@P3: new endowment fund to support visiting scientists\nL412@P3: and scholars. The Infosys Fund has been created\nL413@P3: with a $2 million gift to support two scholars across\nL414@P3: the Institute’s four Schools each year. The Infosys\nL415@P3: Members will receive support related to all aspects of\nL416@P3: the academic appointment and community life.\nL417@P3: Infosys has a longstanding connection with the\nL418@P3: Institute through the Infosys Prize, which has recog\u0002nized several of its former visiting scientists in the\nL419@P3: subject of mathematical sciences.\nL420@P3: “We believe that people, when amplified by tech\u0002nology, knowledge, imagination, and an enriching\nL421@P3: eco system, can achieve incredible things,” said Vishal\nL422@P3: Sikka, Chief Executive Officer and Managing Direc\u0002tor of Infosys. “This is the beginning of a human rev\u0002olution, and institutions like IAS have long served to\nL423@P3: accelerate it. By mentoring visiting scholars, in both\nL424@P3: academic and practical terms, the IAS has created yet\nL425@P3: another avenue to produce advances in knowledge\nL426@P3: that can change the way we work and live—with\nL427@P3: wide-ranging impact on global development.” ■\nL428@P3: Eric Schmidt hosted an IAS- Google event in February\nL429@P3: 2014, “Imagining the Future,” which explored the\nL430@P3:  frontiers of science and technology.\nL431@P3: DAN KOMODA\nL432@P3: Curiosities\nL433@P3: Light’s Revelations and Mysteries\nL434@P3: Light connects us to the very beginning of the uni verse. The\nL435@P3: first light was emitted roughly 380,000 years after the Big\nL436@P3: Bang, when matter was no longer closely tied together and\n"
